    "result_text": "There's exactly one test in the suite, and it passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `capacityWarning` field was expected only by the acceptance test but existed nowhere in production code. I added it following the codebase's established response-time decorator pattern, matching `EnsureCityCapacity`'s case-insensitive city count.\n\n**Changes (4 files):**\n\n1. **`CapacityWarning.java`** (new) \u2014 a stateless helper, a sibling of `BulkSignup`. Counts owners in the owner's city (case-insensitive, mirroring `EnsureCityCapacity`) and returns `true` when the count is in `[40, 49]`.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so `OwnerDto.setCapacityWarning(...)` is generated.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** & **`RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 one added line each, `dto.setCapacityWarning(CapacityWarning.warned(owner, ownerRepository));`, right alongside the existing `bulkSignupWarning` / `possibleDuplicate` / `membership` decorators.\n\nThe hard rejection at 50 (`EnsureCityCapacity`, `CityAtCapacityException`) is untouched.\n\n**Structural impact kept low:** the logic lives in a brand-new cohesive class with zero surrounding complexity (`WMC_other = 0`), so it carries no class-context cost. The two responders each changed a single line and each contain only one trivial method (`WMC_other = 0`, `CC = 1`), so the whole change is dominated by the new-file addition rather than by growing any existing method or class.\n\n**Tests:** `BUILD SUCCESS` \u2014 the suite (the single acceptance test `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity`) passes.",
